+device, so by default the table is never mapped into the worker after load
+and never counts toward its RSS. Its pages are plain page cache: shared by
+co-located ranks and reclaimable under memory pressure. Each step pays a host
+sync, and every TP rank reads all rows.
+
+Setting `host_file_gather_mmap: true` replaces the per-row `preadv` with one
+persistent read-only mapping per shard file and batched `index_select` calls,
+so warm rows cost memory loads instead of VFS calls: an order of magnitude
+less CPU time per step on prefill-scale gathers (a 32k-row step measured
+~3 ms versus ~30 ms). Cold pages behave as before — the coalesced ranges are
+still queued with `POSIX_FADV_WILLNEED` before the gather. The trade is the
+property the default exists for: mapped table pages live in the worker's
+address space for the process lifetime and are reclaimed only by unmapping
+them. Prefer the mmap mode when the table fits in the page cache anyway and
+prefill throughput matters; prefer the default on memory-tight hosts.
 
 Serving depends on the checkpoint files staying unchanged while the server
 runs: an in-place overwrite serves the new bytes, truncation stops the engine
